{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Build Reliance's P&L Line by Line and Compute Five Margins",
  "scenario": "You're a junior analyst at a Mumbai brokerage and have been handed Reliance Industries' unaudited P&L summary for FY23–FY25. Your desk lead asks you to rebuild the full P&L statement row by row, compute the five key margins (gross, EBITDA, EBIT, PBT, net), and identify which margin compressed most over the period. This is your first real model — read the staircase before you forecast it.",
  "instructions": "In the 'Answers' sheet, reconstruct Reliance's complete P&L for FY25 using the given partial data and margins. Compute all five profit margins as percentages of revenue from operations. Identify the year in which net margin was lowest.",
  "learning_objective": "Reconstruct a full P&L line by line, compute and compare the five profit margins over time, and explain a margin compression by identifying which expense line widened relative to revenue.",
  "hints": [
    "Gross profit always equals Revenue from operations minus COGS. Check your staircase order: Gross → EBITDA → EBIT → PBT → PAT.",
    "Margin = profit ÷ Revenue from operations (never total income). All five margins will be percentages; scale them so 0.18 displays as 18% or store as 18 and divide by 100 in your formula.",
    "Net margin is PAT ÷ Revenue from operations. Compare FY23, FY24, FY25 net margins side by side; the year with the smallest percentage is your answer. PAT = (Revenue − COGS − Opex − Depreciation − Interest) × (1 − tax rate), or use the PBT row and subtract tax."
  ],
